      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Here is a concise summary of the key points and the holding in the case of Sterling v. Maritime Logistics Ltd:\n\nThe court upheld the lower court's ruling that a logistics provider's \"supervening inevitability\" clause regarding weather-related cargo damage was not applicable, and the proximate duty of care remained constant regardless of environmental variables. The court relied on the precedent in Donoghue v Stevenson [1932] AC 562, and interpreted the statutory frameworks of the Merchant Shipping Act 1995 in conjunction with the specific contractual indemnities in Maritime Holdings v Global Trade [2015] EWHC 124 (Comm). The court's holding is that foreseeability is the pivot upon which liability turns, and not the invocability of the \"force majeure\" clause.",
