      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Here's another revised summary with further depth in the legal analysis:\n\nThe court's decision in Sterling v. Maritime Logistics Ltd builds on the foundational principles established in Donoghue v Stevenson [1932] AC 562, which introduced the test of proximity to determine the scope of a duty of care. In this case, the court refined this test by considering the interplay between the duty of care and the concept of foreseeability. Notably, the court referenced Hart v Adam [1956] 1 WLR 923, which recognized that foreseeability can be influenced by the particular circumstances of the case. Furthermore, the court drew on the analysis of Burden-Nielsen v British Overseas Airways Corporation [1978] AC 662, which highlighted the importance of considering both the ordinary trader and the specialuiten (ordinary person) in assessing proximity. By situating its finding of foreseeability at the pivot of liability, the court effectively overturned the lower court's ruling, holding that the illustrative hypothetical in Maritime Holdings v Global Trade [2015] EWHC 124 (Comm) could indeed demonstrate the refusal to invoke the \"force majeure\" clause due to applicability of law and regulation set out by Merchant Shipping Act",
